# Naming & Style Conventions

Scope note: KISS/DRY/YAGNI, "does this need to exist," and simplification
are `ponytail`'s job — always active, don't duplicate it here. This skill
only covers naming and casing.

## Casing: idiomatic per language, always

Never import one language's naming convention into another. Follow the
ecosystem, every time:

| Language/context | Variables/functions | Types/classes | Constants |
|---|---|---|---|
| JS/TS | camelCase | PascalCase | UPPER_SNAKE_CASE (true constants) or camelCase |
| Swift | camelCase | PascalCase | camelCase (Swift convention, not UPPER_SNAKE) |
| Kotlin | camelCase | PascalCase | UPPER_SNAKE_CASE for top-level/companion consts |
| Python | snake_case | PascalCase | UPPER_SNAKE_CASE |
| Go | camelCase (unexported) / PascalCase (exported) | PascalCase | camelCase or PascalCase per export need |
| Rust | snake_case | PascalCase | UPPER_SNAKE_CASE |
| CSS/HTML/URLs | kebab-case | — | — |

If a project's existing codebase already deviates from the idiomatic
convention, match the existing codebase over the table — consistency
within a project wins over the abstract ideal.

## Function naming: verb-noun shape

Functions describe an action — name them as one:

```
// GOOD
fetchMarketData(marketId)
calculateSimilarity(a, b)
isValidEmail(email)          // predicate: is/has/can prefix

// BAD
market(id)          // noun only, unclear it's an action
similarity(a, b)     // same
email(e)             // ambiguous — getter? validator? sender?
```

Boolean-returning functions/variables get an `is`/`has`/`can`/`should`
prefix (`isAuthenticated`, `hasPermission`) — the name should make the
boolean nature obvious without checking the return type.

## Variable naming: descriptive over short

No single-letter or abbreviated names outside genuinely conventional scope
(loop indices `i`/`j` in a tight loop, `e` for a caught exception, `_` for
an intentionally unused value). Everything else earns a name that states
what it holds:

```
// GOOD
const marketSearchQuery = 'election'
const isUserAuthenticated = true

// BAD
const q = 'election'
const flag = true
```

## File naming

Match the ecosystem's own file-naming convention (kebab-case for most
JS/TS/web projects, snake_case for Python, PascalCase for Swift/Kotlin
type-per-file) — same idiomatic-per-language principle as casing above,
not a fixed personal rule.

## Limitations

- This governs naming only. For whether a variable/function should be
  immutable, for simplification, and for whether code should exist at
  all, defer to `ponytail` — don't re-litigate those concerns here.
